    <div class="row g-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="learn-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Friendly Introduction</h2>
                <p>Many freshers feel microservices are "only for big companies." Actually, the concept is simple. A microservices application is like a team of focused specialists, not one giant all-in-one worker. Each service owns one business capability and collaborates with others through APIs or events.</p>
                <p class="mb-0">By the end of this lesson, you will be able to explain microservices confidently in classes, projects, and interviews.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="learn-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">This post covers</h2>
                <div>
                    <span class="pill">Definition & core idea</span>
                    <span class="pill">Characteristics</span>
                    <span class="pill">Communication styles</span>
                    <span class="pill">Monolith vs microservices</span>
                    <span class="pill">E-commerce & OTT examples</span>
                    <span class="pill">ASP.NET Core architecture</span>
                    <span class="pill">MCQs + Interview Q&amp;A</span>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="learn-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Step-by-Step: Definition, Core Idea, and Characteristics</h2>
                <h3 class="h5">Step 1 - Definition of Microservices</h3>
                <p>Microservices is an architectural style where one application is built as many small, independent services. Each service does one business job well, can be deployed independently, and communicates through API-based communication.</p>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-3">Step 2 - Core Idea Behind Microservices</h3>
                <p>Now let us compare this with a monolithic application: in monolith, all modules ship together; in microservices, each capability can evolve independently. This supports faster releases and independent development teams.</p>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-3">Step 3 - Characteristics of Microservices</h3>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Small and independent services:</strong> each service has focused scope.</li>
                    <li><strong>Single responsibility:</strong> one service, one clear purpose.</li>
                    <li><strong>Service per business capability:</strong> cart, payment, recommendations, etc.</li>
                    <li><strong>Independent deployment:</strong> release one service without redeploying all.</li>
                    <li><strong>Independent teams:</strong> each team owns one or more services end-to-end.</li>
                    <li><strong>Decentralized data management:</strong> database per service concept.</li>
                    <li><strong>Loose coupling + high cohesion:</strong> services coordinate, but stay autonomous.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12 col-lg-6">
            <div class="learn-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h5 section-title">Monolith vs Microservices Overview</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">Monolith
[UI + Orders + Payments + Catalog + DB]  --> One deployable unit

Microservices
[API Gateway]
  |--> [Catalog Service] --> [Catalog DB]
  |--> [Cart Service]    --> [Cart DB]
  |--> [Order Service]   --> [Order DB]
  |--> [Payment Service] --> [Payment DB]</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12 col-lg-6">
            <div class="learn-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h5 section-title">Structure of a Microservices Application</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">Client -> API Gateway -> Service A/B/C
                       -> Identity Service
                       -> Notification Service
Every service has:
- own code
- own CI/CD
- own data store
- own monitoring metrics/logs</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="learn-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Visual Concepts (Project-Safe Diagrams)</h2>
<pre class="diagram-box">1) Service Per Business Capability
   [User] [Catalog] [Cart] [Order] [Payment] [Shipping] [Notification]

2) Database Per Service
   Catalog Service -> CatalogDB
   Order Service   -> OrderDB
   Payment Service -> PaymentDB

3) Independent Deployment
   Deploy Catalog v2.1 without touching Payment v1.8

4) Service Communication Flow
